To interface a 5 V sensor output with an nRF52840 SAADC input, you must use a resistor divider to step down the voltage. Connecting a 5 V signal directly to an nRF52840 pin violates its absolute maximum and operating voltage ratings [nRF52840_PS p.72, 73, 135].

---

### 1. Resistor Divider Design

The nRF52840 operates with a maximum recommended supply voltage $V_{DD}$ of 3.6 V [nRF52840_PS p.73]. The maximum allowed voltage on any GPIO pin is $V_{DD} + 0.3\text{ V}$ [nRF52840_PS p.135]. Furthermore, the nRF52840 SAADC has a maximum input range (using internal references and gain settings) that typically spans up to 3.6 V or lower.

To safely step down a 0–5 V sensor output:
* Choose a target maximum input voltage for the nRF52840 analog pin—for example, **3.0 V** (which fits well within standard SAADC input ranges).
* Select standard resistor values to achieve a division factor of $\frac{3.0\text{ V}}{5.0\text{ V}} = 0.6$ (or a ratio of $3:5$).

Using standard 1% tolerance resistors:
* **$R_{top}$ (connected to 5V Sensor):** $20\text{ k}\Omega$
* **$R_{bottom}$ (connected to nRF52840 SAADC Pin and Ground):** $30\text{ k}\Omega$

$$\text{V}_{\text{ADC}} = 5.0\text{ V} \times \left(\frac{30\text{ k}\Omega}{20\text{ k}\Omega + 30\text{ k}\Omega}\right) = 5.0\text{ V} \times 0.6 = 3.0\text{ V}$$

*To minimize loading on your 5V sensor while keeping source impedance low enough for accurate ADC sampling (recommended $< 10\text{ k}\Omega$), you can also scale these values up or down (e.g., $R_{top} = 200\text{ k}\Omega$, $R_{bottom} = 300\text{ k}\Omega$, though higher source impedances require longer SAADC acquisition times).*

---

### 2. SAADC Channel Configuration

To measure the scaled 0–3.0 V signal accurately, configure the SAADC channel as follows:

* **Input Mode:** Single-ended (`NRF_SAADC_INPUT_AIN0` through `AIN7`, corresponding to the chosen GPIO pin).
* **Reference Voltage:** Internal reference ($0.6\text{ V}$).
* **Gain:** `1/6` (which scales the $0.6\text{ V}$ internal reference to an input range of $0.6\text{ V} / (1/6) = 3.6\text{ V}0$). With a 3.0 V max input, this utilizes most of the ADC range without clipping.
* **Acquisition Time (Sampling Time):** Set to `4us` or higher (increase if using higher resistor divider values to allow the sample-and-hold capacitor to charge fully).
* **Resolution:** 12-bit (default) or 14-bit oversampled.

---

### 3. Firmware Configuration Example (Nordic nRF Connect SDK / Zephyr)

```c
#include <zephyr/kernel.h>
#include <zephyr/drivers/adc.h>

/* Define the ADC channel configuration for SAADC */
#define ADC_NODE DT_NODELABEL(saadc)

static const struct adc_channel_cfg channel_cfg = {
    .gain       = ADC_GAIN_1_6,              /* Gain 1/6: Input range 0 to 3.6V (with 0.6V internal ref) */
    .reference  = ADC_REF_INTERNAL,          /* Use internal 0.6V reference */
    .acquisition_time = ADC_ACQ_TIME(ADC_ACQ_TIME_MICROSECONDS, 10), /* 10 us acquisition time */
    .channel_id = 0,                         /* Channel 0 */
    .input_positive = SAADC_CH_PSELP_PSELP_AnalogInput0, /* AIN0 / P0.02 */
};

void configure_saadc(void) {
    const struct device *adc_dev = DEVICE_DT_GET(ADC_NODE);
    if (!device_is_ready(adc_dev)) {
        return;
    }

    adc_channel_setup(adc_dev, &channel_cfg);
}
```

